
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third question — the delivery mechanism, since this repo has no build step today.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the new validation files get loaded by the browser. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m today is a bare [38;5;153m<script src>[39m defining
  globals; [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS but is unrelated to the webapp. Splitting validation into its own file forces a choice.

  - [1mES modules, no build step[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m between files. Native in every
    current browser, and Node can run the same files for tests with zero tooling. Cost: [38;5;153mfile://[39m opening of [38;5;153mindex.html[39m
    stops working (modules need an HTTP origin), so you'd serve the page locally.
  - [1mGlobals via extra [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m assigns [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m. Nothing else changes and [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps
    working. Cost: load-order dependencies, no real import graph, and testing means faking a [38;5;153mwindow[39m.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — Vite or esbuild. Buys you the whole modern toolchain. Cost: a dependency tree and config for a
    project that currently has zero dependencies.

  Cheap to change later: adding files inside whichever scheme you pick. Expensive: switching schemes later touches every
  file and the HTML.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules, no build step.[0m It keeps the zero-dependency property, gives the validation core a clean
  import surface, and makes the core unit-testable in Node without a DOM. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m loss is the only real cost and
  [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m covers it.

